- Tailings behaves as predicted and flows down the Senunu Canyon to depths >3,000 m; 

- No tailings have been observed rising above into the surface mixed layer; 

- No evidence of any elevated concentration of metals in fish tissue; 

- Reduced abundances of benthic fauna in deposited tailings; 

- Some tailings are detectable (by some identifying criteria) settling from the subsurface plumes, extending laterally from the density current / accumulation footprint. This was expected but could not be modeled at the time of impact assessment. 

- Elevated dissolved copper, turbidity and TSS are found only at the bottom waters of the canyon above the tailings density current, within the permitted mixing zone. Outside the mixing zone, the water quality remains at background levels. Concentrations of most dissolved metals were in sub μg/L levels. 

The DSTP system at Batu Hijau was approved and designed prior to the development of the SAMS guidelines. The only unexpected impact was the excessive wear of the pipeline, requiring more frequent changeout that planned. A dual system is now in place allowing operations to continue while maintenance and monitoring of pipeline wear is undertaken with a dedicated ultrasonic system. The environmental performance has achieved the objectives of protection embodied in the SAMS guidelines and this operation can be considered the benchmark current best practice for any large copper mine considering DSTP, in terms of engineering safeguards and environmental monitoring. 

_Proceeding of Copper 2013 Santiago, Chile_ 

233 

#### **SUMMARY – APPLICATION TO OTHER AREAS** 

The independent study carried out by SAMS in 2007/08 was a PNG initiative and to that extent, the draft guidelines reflect PNG conditions, which are characterized primarily by the archipelagic settings of the Asia Pacific region.  Where the essential physical criteria of discharge depth, bathymetric gradient and absence of upwelling influences are met, as at operations such as Misima, Lihir (in PNG) and Batu Hijau in Indonesia [8, 9, 13], surface waters have not been adversely affected by tailings reentrainment and impacts have generally been as predicted and as permitted. The SAMS draft guidelines are now applied (by the regulators) to impact assessments for projects contemplating DSTP as a tailings management option in PNG. This should assure the same standards are applied and outcomes achieved for new projects in PNG considering DSTP. 

